{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019GttzGLmBubxpjbKoKTJ7J","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/* SPDX-License-Identifier: SunMicrosystems */\n2\t/* Copyright (C) 1993 by Sun Microsystems, Inc. All rights reserved. */\n3\t\n4\t/**\n5\t *\n6\t * This family of functions is a set of functions used by the gamma function\n7\t * procedures as internal functions. Only ``__lgamma`` is accessible via\n8\t * ``gammad.h`` (``gammaf.h``), but should not be accessed directly by a user.\n9\t *\n10\t * Synopsis\n11\t * ========\n12\t *\n13\t * .. code-block:: c\n14\t *\n15\t *     #include <math.h>\n16\t *     float __sin_pif(float x)\n17\t *     double __sin_pi(double x)\n18\t *     float __lgammaf(float x, int *signgamp)\n19\t *     double __lgamma(double x, int *signgamp)\n20\t *\n21\t * Description\n22\t * ===========\n23\t *\n24\t * ``__lgamma`` computes the natural logarithm of the gamma function of\n25\t * :math:`x` and places the sign of the gamma function in the out-pointer\n26\t * :math:`signgamp`.\n27\t *\n28\t * ``__sin_pi`` computes the sine of the input value after it was multiplied by\n29\t * :math:`\\pi`. The procedure is only called in the range\n30\t * :math:`[-2^{52},-2^{-70}]`, calling it outside of this range may result in\n31\t * unexpected results.\n32\t *\n33\t * Mathematical Function\n34\t * =====================\n35\t *\n36\t * .. math::\n37\t *\n38\t *    \\_\\_sin\\_pi(x) &\\approx sin(\\pi \\cdot x)  \\\\\n39\t *    \\_\\_lgamma(x) &\\approx \\ln{|\\Gamma(x)|} = \\ln{\\left|\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-t}t^{x-1}dt\\right|}  \\\\\n40\t *    \\_\\_lgamma\\_signgamp(x) &= \\left\\{\\begin{array}{ll} +1, & +0 \\leq  \\Gamma(x) \\\\ -1, & -0 \\geq \\Gamma(x) \\end{array}\\right.\n41\t *\n42\t * Returns\n43\t * =======\n44\t *\n45\t * ``__lgamma`` returns the natural logarithm of the gamma function of\n46\t * :math:`x` and places the sign of the gamma function in the out-pointer\n47\t * :math:`signgamp`.\n48\t *\n49\t * ``__sin_pi`` returns the sine of :math:`\\pi \\cdot x`.\n50\t *\n51\t * Exceptions\n52\t * ==========\n53\t *\n54\t * Do not raise useful exceptions.\n55\t *\n56\t * .. May raise ``underflow`` exception.\n57\t *\n58\t * Output map\n59\t * ==========\n60\t *\n61\t * The output maps are in the respective external functions :ref:`lgamma` and\n62\t * :ref:`tgamma`.\n63\t *\n64\t */\n65\t\n66\t/* __lgamma_r(x, signgamp)\n67\t * Reentrant version of the logarithm of the Gamma function\n68\t * with user provide pointer for the sign of Gamma(x).\n69\t *\n70\t * Method:\n71\t *   1. Argument Reduction for 0 < x <= 8\n72\t *     Since gamma(1+s)=s*gamma(s), for x in [0,8], we may\n73\t *     reduce x to a number in [1.5,2.5] by\n74\t *         lgamma(1+s) = log(s) + lgamma(s)\n75\t *    for example,\n76\t *        lgamma(7.3) = log(6.3) + lgamma(6.3)\n77\t *                = log(6.3*5.3) + lgamma(5.3)\n78\t *                = log(6.3*5.3*4.3*3.3*2.3) + lgamma(2.3)\n79\t *   2. Polynomial approximation of lgamma around its\n80\t *    minimum ymin=1.461632144968362245 to maintain monotonicity.\n81\t *    On [ymin-0.23, ymin+0.27] (i.e., [1.23164,1.73163]), use\n82\t *        Let z = x-ymin;\n83\t *        lgamma(x) = -1.214862905358496078218 + z^2*poly(z)\n84\t *    where\n85\t *        poly(z) is a 14 degree polynomial.\n86\t *   2. Rational approximation in the primary interval [2,3]\n87\t *    We use the following approximation:\n88\t *        s = x-2.0;\n89\t *        lgamma(x) = 0.5*s + s*P(s)/Q(s)\n90\t *    with accuracy\n91\t *        |P/Q - (lgamma(x)-0.5s)| < 2**-61.71\n92\t *    Our algorithms are based on the following observation\n93\t *\n94\t *                             zeta(2)-1    2    zeta(3)-1    3\n95\t * lgamma(2+s) = s*(1-Euler) + --------- * s  -  --------- * s  + ...\n96\t *                                 2                 3\n97\t *\n98\t *    where Euler = 0.5771... is the Euler constant, which is very\n99\t *    close to 0.5.\n100\t *\n101\t *   3. For x